Task 1: Subscribe to OpenShift Virtualization Operator
Subtask 1.1: Access the OpenShift Web Console
Open your web browser and navigate to the OpenShift web console URL provided in your lab environment.

Log in using the credentials provided:

Username: admin
Password: [provided in lab environment]
Verify cluster access by checking the cluster status in the top-right corner of the console.

Subtask 1.2: Navigate to OperatorHub
Click on Operators in the left navigation menu.

Select OperatorHub from the dropdown menu.

Search for OpenShift Virtualization in the search bar at the top of the page.

Locate the OpenShift Virtualization operator - it should display with a Red Hat logo and official certification.

Subtask 1.3: Install the Operator via Web Console
Click on the OpenShift Virtualization tile to open the operator details page.

Review the operator information:

Verify the operator version (should be 4.12 or later)
Check the supported OpenShift versions
Review the operator capabilities
Click Install to begin the installation process.

Configure installation settings:

Installation Mode: Select "All namespaces on the cluster"
Installed Namespace: Choose "openshift-cnv" (will be created automatically)
Update Channel: Select "stable"
Approval Strategy: Choose "Automatic"
Click Install to confirm the installation.

Subtask 1.4: Install the Operator via CLI (Alternative Method)
If you prefer using the command line, follow these steps:

Open a terminal in your lab environment.

Create the operator namespace:

oc create namespace openshift-cnv
Create the OperatorGroup:

cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged-group
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - openshift-cnv
EOF
Create the Subscription:

cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: hco-operatorhub
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged
channel: "stable"
installPlanApproval: Automatic
EOF
Task 2: Install Required CRDs
Subtask 2.1: Verify Operator Installation
Check the operator installation status:

oc get csv -n openshift-cnv
Wait for the operator to reach "Succeeded" phase:

oc get csv -n openshift-cnv -w
Expected Output:

NAME                                      DISPLAY                    VERSION   REPLACES   PHASE
kubevirt-hyperconverged-operator.v4.12.0   OpenShift Virtualization   4.12.0               Succeeded
Verify operator pods are running:

oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
Subtask 2.2: Create HyperConverged Custom Resource
Create the HyperConverged CR to install all required CRDs and components:

cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: hco.kubevirt.io/v1beta1
kind: HyperConverged
metadata:
  name: kubevirt-hyperconverged
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  BareMetalPlatform: true
EOF
Monitor the HyperConverged resource creation:

oc get hco -n openshift-cnv kubevirt-hyperconverged -w
Subtask 2.3: Verify CRD Installation
List all OpenShift Virtualization CRDs:

oc get crd | grep -E "(kubevirt|cdi|hco|ssp|nmstate)"
Verify specific important CRDs:

oc get crd virtualmachines.kubevirt.io
oc get crd virtualmachineinstances.kubevirt.io
oc get crd datavolumes.cdi.kubevirt.io
oc get crd hyperconvergeds.hco.kubevirt.io
Check CRD details for the VirtualMachine resource:

oc describe crd virtualmachines.kubevirt.io
Task 3: Monitor the Operator Deployment
Subtask 3.1: Monitor Component Deployment
Check all namespaces created by OpenShift Virtualization:

oc get namespaces | grep -E "(cnv|kubevirt|cdi|ssp|nmstate)"
Monitor pods in the openshift-cnv namespace:

oc get pods -n openshift-cnv -w
Check pods in kubevirt system namespaces:

oc get pods -n kubevirt-hostpath-provisioner
oc get pods -n openshift-nmstate
Subtask 3.2: Verify Core Components
Check KubeVirt deployment:

oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv
Verify CDI (Containerized Data Importer) status:

oc get cdi -n openshift-cnv
Check SSP (Scheduling, Scale and Performance) operator:

oc get ssp -n openshift-cnv
Verify NetworkAddonsConfig:

oc get networkaddonsconfig -n openshift-cnv
Subtask 3.3: Comprehensive Status Check
Create a comprehensive status check script:

cat << 'EOF' > check-cnv-status.sh
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== OpenShift Virtualization Status Check ==="
echo

echo "1. Checking HyperConverged status:"
oc get hco -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "2. Checking operator CSV status:"
oc get csv -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "3. Checking core component status:"
echo "KubeVirt:"
oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv
echo "CDI:"
oc get cdi -n openshift-cnv
echo "SSP:"
oc get ssp -n openshift-cnv
echo "NetworkAddonsConfig:"
oc get networkaddonsconfig -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "4. Checking pod status in openshift-cnv:"
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
echo

echo "5. Checking if virtualization is ready:"
if oc get hco -n openshift-cnv kubevirt-hyperconverged -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Available")].status}' | grep -q "True"; then
    echo "✓ OpenShift Virtualization is READY"
else
    echo "✗ OpenShift Virtualization is NOT READY"
fi
EOF

chmod +x check-cnv-status.sh
Run the status check script:

./check-cnv-status.sh
Subtask 3.4: Verify Node Preparation
Check if nodes are prepared for virtualization:

oc get nodes -o wide
Verify KubeVirt handler daemonset:

oc get daemonset -n openshift-cnv
Check node labels for virtualization:

oc get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.metadata.labels.kubevirt\.io/schedulable}{"\n"}{end}'
